I didn't add the `last-input` write. The node's `with` clause grants exactly three interfaces: `http-client`, `notifier`, and `wall-clock`. Recording to the store needs `aap:caps/state-rw`, which isn't among them, and the approval text doesn't change the grant. Adding that import would give the node authority its contract doesn't declare. If the platform team extends the `with` clause, the world can add `import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;` and the body can call `put("last-input", &feed_url)`.

Separately, `ensures notified == true` holds only if `notify` returns `true`. The body reports whatever the notifier returns and does not force the field to `true`, so a rejected notification will violate the contract.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client::fetch;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier::notify;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock::now;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = now().seconds;

        let fetched = match fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!(
            "heartbeat {seconds}: fetched {fetched} chars from {feed_url}"
        );
        let notified = notify(&message);

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

